Sure! If you already have your data in a variable called `myItemList`, you can let the helper function do the heavy lifting.  
Just feed it the array (as a JSON‑string), tell it that `"urgent"` is the priority status, and set the `ascending` flag to `"true"`.

```javascript
// Assuming myItemList looks like this:
const myItemList = [
  { name: "Alpha", status: "normal" },
  { name: "Bravo", status: "urgent" },
  { name: "Charlie", status: "normal" },
  { name: "Delta", status: "urgent" }
];

// Call the helper
const sorted = await functions.prioritizeAndSort({
  items: JSON.stringify(myItemList),
  priorityStatus: "urgent",
  ascending: "true"
});

console.log(sorted);
```

The helper will:

1. Pull out all items whose `status` is `"urgent"` and put them first (in the order they appear in the original array).  
2. Sort the remaining items alphabetically by their `name` property in ascending order.  

If you run the snippet above, the output will be:

```json
[
  { "name": "Bravo", "status": "urgent" },
  { "name": "Delta", "status": "urgent" },
  { "name": "Alpha", "status": "normal" },
  { "name": "Charlie", "status": "normal" }
]
```

Feel free to replace the sample data with your own `myItemList`. Let me know if you’d like help wiring this into your existing codebase!
